      "notes": [
        "**कितने का है? — why का, and why कितने?** A Hindi price question literally says \"this is of how much?\" — the item is *worth* a sum of money. The form follows the item: *यह **कितने का** है?* (masculine singular), *यह **कितने की** है?* (feminine), *ये **कितने के** हैं?* (plural). ⚠️ When in doubt, use a safe phrase that never changes: **इसका दाम क्या है?** or **कितना हुआ?**",
        "**Hindi numbers can't be built from parts — each one is its own word.** English makes \"thirty-five\" from two pieces you already know. Hindi has **पैंतीस**, one whole word that sounds like neither तीन nor पाँच. ⚠️ The first hundred numbers have to be learned one by one, and there's no shortcut. The good news: you've memorized a hundred other things in your life, and numbers are used many times a day, so they stick faster than you'd think.",
        "**A hint that still helps: the unit comes IN FRONT of the ten.** इक·तीस (31), बत्तीस (32), पैं·तालीस (45), छि·हत्तर (76). You can't guess the exact form, but once you know **तीस, चालीस, पचास, साठ, सत्तर, अस्सी, नब्बे**, you can **recognize** numbers you hear even before you can produce them. Recognize first, produce later.",
        "**रुपये is the plural of रुपया.** *एक रुपया*, but *चालीस रुपये*. ⚠️ There's no slang shortcut like \"bucks\" or \"k\"; Indians say the number in full, or drop रुपये altogether: *चालीस* alone is understood as forty rupees.",
        "**Bargaining is a conversation, not a fight.** At open markets, street stalls, and souvenir shops, the first price is meant to be bargained. **Not** bargained: shops with price tags, supermarkets, restaurants, pharmacies, and metered taxis. Phrases that work: **थोड़ा कम कीजिए** (bring it down a little), **कुछ कम करो भैया** (a bit less, bhaiya), or simply repeating the price in a shocked voice like Ani. ⚠️ Bargaining with a smile works; bargaining angrily doesn't.",
        "**छुट्टा — the word that will save you every day.** It means \"small change\", and running short of change is an everyday problem in India. Ask **छुट्टा है?** before handing over a big note, and keep ten- and twenty-rupee notes on you. Rickshaw drivers and tea sellers often really don't have change; they aren't dodging.",
        "**आइएगा in the last line isn't a typo.** The **-गा** ending on a polite command turns it into a gentle invitation for later: *आइए* = \"come in (now)\", *आइएगा* = \"do come again (sometime)\". It's very characteristic and sounds very friendly; you don't need to use it yet, but recognize it when you hear it."
      ],
