        {
          "type": "kotak",
          "title": "Dietary needs",
          "items": [
            "**क्या यह हलाल है?** — a question understood all over India; halal meat is easy to find in any city, and shops that sell it usually show **हलाल** on their signboard.",
            "**मैं सूअर का माँस नहीं खाती।** (*maiⁿ suu-ar kaa maaⁿs na-hiiⁿ khaa-tii*) — \"I don't eat pork.\" In most of India this is hardly ever an issue: pork rarely appears in ordinary eateries.",
            "**मैं शाकाहारी हूँ** (*shaa-kaa-haa-rii*) means \"I'm vegetarian\" — a very useful word, because vegetarian restaurants are everywhere and their vegetarian menus are often the best. Look for a **green** dot on packaging; a **red** dot means it contains meat or egg.",
            "⚠️ Worth knowing: in many parts of India **beef isn't sold at all**, because the cow is revered in Hinduism. Don't ask for it in an ordinary restaurant; it's a matter of sensitivity, not availability."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "A way to practice numbers that really works: every time you see a number today — a house number, a price on the checkout screen, the time on your phone — say it in Hindi, aloud if you can. Ten seconds, twenty times a day. That works far better than an hour spent memorizing a table, because numbers have to come out **fast**, not be slowly recalled."
        }
      ]
